\subsection{Evidence Strategy Comparison}
\label{sec:evidence_strategy}

We compare joint generation against post-hoc retrieval, using Evi-F1 (joint) with the DREEAM-protocol denominator~\citep{ma2023dreeam} for fair cross-strategy comparison. Post-hoc BM25 retrieval (entity-constrained, $k{=}2$ sentences per triple) achieves higher Rel-F1 (48.76) by avoiding the evidence tax, but produces lower Evi-F1 (joint) of 34.7 with EDCR 0.766. RSFT CED (s43) reaches Evi-F1 (joint) 36.1 with EDCR 0.654, a 1.4-point evidence advantage and 0.112 EDCR reduction. CED reranking at inference time (selecting the best of $N{=}8$ outputs by CED score) achieves Rel-F1 48.64 and Evi-F1 81.88 (TP-only), approaching no-evidence extraction quality while preserving evidence. Joint training produces more faithful evidence grounded in the extraction reasoning rather than retrieved post-hoc; inference-time reranking complements this by recovering extraction accuracy.


\subsection{Generalization}
\label{sec:generalization}

Cross-architecture validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms the scaling patterns: the evidence tax at 8B is near zero ($-0.68$ points), and RSFT CED yields +3.30 Ign-F1 gain. The RSFT improvement transfers consistently across datasets: on Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred}, RSFT achieves 41.45 Ign-F1 (+4.86pp over SFT baseline 36.59), closely matching the +4.92pp gain on DocRED. Evidence quality remains stable (Evi-F1 81.82 vs.\ 81.70 for SFT) with improved coupling (EDCR 0.6210 vs.\ 0.6674). Our best joint Evi-F1 (36.1, DREEAM-protocol denominator) falls below supervised DREEAM (54.01\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), a gap driven by extraction performance rather than evidence quality (TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%). Full results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:generalization_full}.

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison on Qwen3-4B. The SFT baseline exhibits high seed sensitivity: three-seed mean Ign-F1 is 43.58 with standard deviation 3.80pp (individual seeds range from 39.74 to 47.34). This variance is comparable to the evidence tax itself ($-3.63$ points), indicating that single-seed evaluation can substantially misestimate the true cost of joint evidence generation. Removing evidence entirely (no-evidence SFT) reaches 47.21 Ign-F1.

RSFT CED achieves a three-seed mean of 44.82 Ign-F1 (+1.24 over SFT mean) while compressing variance to 0.51pp, a 7.5$\times$ reduction. This stabilization holds across all three Qwen3 scales (Section~\ref{sec:scaling}). Even the weakest RSFT seed (44.23) exceeds the SFT mean (43.58), and every RSFT seed outperforms two of three SFT seeds. Evidence quality improves (Evi-F1 80.96 vs.\ 79.61) and coupling tightens (EDCR 0.6487 vs.\ 0.6797). CED-based selection thus provides two complementary benefits: it raises mean extraction performance and stabilizes it across training runs. Per-seed results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}.

The remaining gap to the no-evidence upper bound (2.39 points) represents the residual cost of producing structured evidence, a cost that varies with model scale (Section~\ref{sec:scaling}).

